    {
      "id": "gqrk59o",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5.3 Languages"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "i5nmibr",
      "content": "Arunachal Pradesh is one of the most linguistically diverse regions on earth. Over 50 languages and hundreds of dialects are spoken in the state. Most belong to the Tibeto-Burman family, with a few from the Tai-Kadai and Indo-Aryan groups."
    },
    {
      "id": "1gtf79e",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Nyishi (Nishing) — most widely spoken tribal language",
        "Adi — language of the Adi tribe; has literature and newspapers",
        "Bengali and Hindi — used for inter-tribal communication",
        "English — official language of the state government",
        "Tibetan — used in Buddhist monasteries in Tawang and West Kameng",
        "Khamti — has its own Tai-based script",
        "Chakma — has its own Brahmic-derived script (Ojhapath)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "cax4efd",
      "content": "Arunachal Pradesh has declared Bengali as a co-official language in some districts. English remains the primary language of administration and education."
    },
