    {
      "id": "pvk4nuh",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5.5 Donyi-Polo — The Indigenous Faith"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "x82txmi",
      "content": "Donyi-Polo (literally 'Sun-Moon') is the indigenous animistic faith of most Adi, Nyishi, Galo, Tagin, Hill Miri, and related tribes. It was formalised as an organised religion in the 1970s–80s to preserve tribal identity against proselytisation. The Donyi-Polo movement emphasises worship of the sun (Donyi) and moon (Polo), reverence for nature, and adherence to tribal customs."
    },
    {
      "id": "jug1p1c",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Donyi-Polo Yelam Kebang (DPYK) is the apex organisation of the faith",
        "It was spearheaded by leaders like Talom Rukbo of Aalo, West Siang",
        "First Donyi-Polo shrine/temple was established at Itanagar",
        "The faith stresses environmental conservation, ancestral respect, and community solidarity"
      ]
    }
  ],
